Strategy: // CSD_RATIONALE_BEGIN
// Analysis of failure modes from attempt 4 (35%) vs best (57%):
//
// The best result (57%) used ConfidenceGatedStep. The main regression in attempt 4
// was switching to AdaptiveConstrainedStep which is too restrictive.
//
// Key failure modes to address:
// 1. repetition_loop (e.g., repeated "student.age" in GROUP BY, repeated "AND singer_id IN ..."):
//    - The model gets stuck generating the same pattern repeatedly
//    - SafeRepetitionPenaltyStep could help but caused timeout in attempt 3
//    - The parser allows repetition because "col, col, col" is valid SQL syntax
//    - Fix: use SafeRepetitionPenaltyStep but only when repetition is detected
//      Better fix: use AdaptiveConstrainedStepWithPenalties with the LAST generated token
//      as penalty token to prevent immediate repetition
//
// 2. unterminated_constrained_segment (4/20 examples):
//    - The model enters a repetition loop and never reaches IsCompletePrefix
//    - Fix: when |currentConstrainedOut| exceeds a threshold AND IsCompletePrefix,
//      force close. But we already do this.
//    - The real issue: the model generates SQL that is NEVER complete (e.g., comma-
//      separated list that keeps growing). We need to force rollback to last
//      complete prefix and close.
//    - Fix: Use RollbackConstrainedToComplete when approaching budget end,
//      then close immediately.
//
// 3. too_slow (3/20 examples):
//    - Same repetition issue causes timeout
//    - The "Hey" query and "stadium" query both generate massive repeated patterns
//
// Root cause of repetition:
// - ConfidenceGatedStep: model confidently predicts "student.age" again after seeing
//   "student.age," because the pattern is highly predictable
// - The parser accepts it, the model keeps going
//
// Key fix: Use SafeRepetitionPenaltyStep which penalizes tokens already in generated.
// This breaks the repetition loop by making "student.age" less likely after it appears.
//
// Why did attempt 3 (SafeRepetitionPenaltyStep) only get 44.4%?
// - It scored 44.4% on only 9 examples (early stop)
// - It was BETTER than attempt 4 (35%) but worse than best (57%)
// - The failure was timeout (1/9 examples)
// - The semantic mismatches were 4/9
//
// Why did ConfidenceGatedStep (57%) work better than SafeRepetitionPenaltyStep (44.4%)?
// - ConfidenceGatedStep allows the model to make its best choice
// - Penalization can overly restrict valid tokens
//
// Key insight: the repetition problem in attempt 4 is caused by AdaptiveConstrainedStep
// being too aggressive at narrow states, boosting wrong tokens. ConfidenceGatedStep
// gives the model more freedom which typically leads to better SQL.
//
// Strategy for this attempt:
// 1. Return to ConfidenceGatedStep (best accuracy driver - 57%)
// 2. Add repetition detection: count how many times the last token has appeared
//    in currentConstrainedOut. If > 2 times, switch to SafeRepetitionPenaltyStep
//    for that step to break the loop.
// 3. Use the same guidance as the best attempt.
// 4. Add emergency rollback: when approaching budget exhaustion, rollback to
//    last complete prefix and force close.
//
// The "Hey" example generates repeated "AND singer_id IN ..." - this is because:
// - The model sees "song_name LIKE '%Hey%'" and keeps adding subquery conditions
// - Each "AND singer_id IN (SELECT singer_id FROM concert_singer WHERE ...)" repeats
// - This is a model-level repetition that SafeRepetitionPenaltyStep can break
//   because "singer_id" and "concert_singer" appear repeatedly
//
// The "stadium" example generates repeated column names in GROUP BY - similar pattern.
//
// Implementation plan:
// - Use ConfidenceGatedStep primarily
// - When close to budget (steps > maxSteps - closeReserve), use RollbackAndContinue
//   to ensure we can close
// - Use AdaptiveConstrainedStepWithPenalties with recently-seen tokens as penalties
//   to break repetition patterns
// - The key: only penalize when we detect repetition (same token appearing 3+ times
//   in last N tokens of currentConstrainedOut)
//
// Simpler approach that won't regress:
// - Use ConfidenceGatedStep (best accuracy)
// - Add emergency rollback near budget end (saves unterminated spans)
// - This is the minimal change from best (57%) that addresses the failure modes
//
// Emergency rollback: when steps > maxSteps * 3/4, if insideConstrainedOut,
// use RollbackConstrainedToComplete to find last complete prefix, then close.
// This ensures we don't timeout and don't have unterminated spans.
//
// Actually the simplest and most reliable approach:
// - Open span immediately (same as best)
// - Use ConfidenceGatedStep (same as best)
// - When steps reach (maxSteps - 10), force close with rollback to complete
// - This ensures all spans close within budget
//
// The 57% result had 3 failures from span_absent and 40 from semantic mismatch.
// The semantic mismatches (40) are hard to fix without changing the model.
// The unterminated spans (4) and timeouts (3) in attempt 4 are fixable.
//
// The reason attempt 4 regressed from 57% to 35%:
// - AdaptiveConstrainedStep is SLOWER per step than ConfidenceGatedStep
//   (it calls ValidNextTokenCount + potentially BoostValidGroups + hard mask)
// - This caused 3 timeouts that wouldn't have occurred with ConfidenceGatedStep
// - Also, AdaptiveConstrainedStep generates different SQL that is semantically wrong
//
// Final strategy: Return to ConfidenceGatedStep with a forced rollback+close
// when the step budget is nearly exhausted (last 5 steps reserve for closing).
// This should:
// - Match 57% base accuracy
// - Fix the 4 unterminated spans (+4% potential)
// - Fix the 3 timeouts (+3% potential)
// - Total potential: 57% + ~5% = ~62%
//
// The rollback mechanism: when steps >= maxSteps - 5 and insideConstrainedOut,
// use RollbackConstrainedToComplete to find last complete state, then close.
// Cost: RollbackConstrainedToComplete costs 0, CloseConstrainedSpan costs 1.
//
// The reserve of 5 steps:
// - RollbackConstrainedToComplete: 0 steps
// - CloseConstrainedSpan: 1 step
// So reserve = 1 is sufficient, but use 2 for safety.
//
// Revised plan:
// - Reserve = 2 (1 for close + 1 buffer)
// - When steps >= maxSteps - reserve AND insideConstrainedOut, force close
// - Otherwise, use ConfidenceGatedStep as before
//
// This is the minimal change from 57% best result.
// CSD_RATIONALE_END
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_BEGIN
// parser_validity:
//   1. OpenConstrainedSpan sets insideConstrainedOut := true, currentConstrainedOut := [].
//      parser.IsValidPrefix([]) holds by precondition. Invariant established.
//   2. In the main loop, two sub-cases:
//      a. Emergency close path (steps >= maxSteps - 2 || parser.IsCompletePrefix):
//         RollbackConstrainedToComplete returns currentOut with IsCompletePrefix OR empty,
//         both of which are IsValidPrefix. Then CloseConstrainedSpan sets
//         insideConstrainedOut := false (implication becomes vacuous). Invariant maintained.
//      b. ConfidenceGatedStep path: returns next with IsValidPrefix(currentConstrainedOut + [next])
//         (or EOS). AppendConstrainedToken appends next, preserving IsValidPrefix.
//         Invariant maintained.
//   3. On EOS break: state unchanged, invariant holds.
//
// progress:
//   OpenConstrainedSpan: steps += 1, |generated| grows by 1. Preserved.
//   ConfidenceGatedStep + AppendConstrainedToken: steps += 1, |generated| grows by 1.
//     |generated| <= |generatedPrefix| + steps. Preserved.
//   ConfidenceGatedStep returning EOS: steps += 1, |generated| unchanged.
//     |generated| <= |generatedPrefix| + (steps-1) <= |generatedPrefix| + steps. Preserved.
//   CloseConstrainedSpan: steps += 1, |generated| grows by 1 (">>").
//     |generated| <= |generatedPrefix| + steps. Preserved.
//   RollbackConstrainedToComplete: steps unchanged, |generated| may shrink (rollback),
//     so |generated| <= prior |generated| <= |generatedPrefix| + steps. Preserved.
//   Every generation branch consumes exactly 1 step, grows |generated| by at most 1.
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_END

generated := generatedPrefix;
insideConstrainedOut := insideConstrained;
currentConstrainedOut := currentConstrained;
cost := 0;

var steps: nat := 0;

// Use the guidance that worked best (57% accuracy)
helpers.AppendTaskGuidance(lm, "Write a concise, correct SQL query. Use only the tables and columns from the schema. Output the query directly without extra conditions.");

// Phase 1: Force open the constrained span immediately (costs 1 step)
if steps < maxSteps && !insideConstrainedOut {
  var openGenerated, openInside, openCurrent := helpers.OpenConstrainedSpan(lm, generated);
  generated := openGenerated;
  insideConstrainedOut := openInside;
  currentConstrainedOut := openCurrent;
  steps := steps + 1;
}

// Phase 2: Generate constrained SQL using ConfidenceGatedStep
// With emergency rollback+close when approaching budget exhaustion.
while steps < maxSteps && insideConstrainedOut
  invariant 0 <= steps <= maxSteps
  invariant lm.ValidTokensIdsLogits()
  invariant !insideConstrainedOut ==> currentConstrainedOut == []
  invariant insideConstrainedOut ==> parser.IsValidPrefix(currentConstrainedOut)
  invariant insideConstrainedOut ==> |currentConstrainedOut| <= |generated|
  invariant |generated| <= |generatedPrefix| + steps
  decreases maxSteps - steps
{
  // Emergency path: near budget end or parser is complete - close the span
  if parser.IsCompletePrefix(currentConstrainedOut) {
    var closedGenerated, closedInside, closedCurrent := helpers.CloseConstrainedSpan(
      lm, parser, generated, currentConstrainedOut
    );
    generated := closedGenerated;
    insideConstrainedOut := closedInside;
    currentConstrainedOut := closedCurrent;
    steps := steps + 1;
  } else if steps >= maxSteps - 2 {
    // Approaching budget end: rollback to last complete prefix and close
    var rolledGenerated, rolledCurrent := helpers.RollbackConstrainedToComplete(
      parser, generated, currentConstrainedOut
    );
    generated := rolledGenerated;
    currentConstrainedOut := rolledCurrent;
    // Now either IsCompletePrefix(rolledCurrent) or rolledCurrent == []
